      "description": "These articles center on CRISPR/Cas9 as a versatile genome-editing platform for both basic research and therapeutic use, with emphasis on how targeted double-strand breaks can be exploited for gene disruption, correction, regulation, and disease modeling. Together they highlight practical strategies to improve editing performance and translational feasibility—such as boosting repair pathways, chemically optimizing guide RNAs, and developing viral or nonviral delivery systems—while reviewing applications in inherited disease, cancer, and other biomedical settings."
