      "representative": {
        "title": "Off‐target assessment of <scp>CRISPR</scp>‐<scp>C</scp>as9 guiding <scp>RNA</scp>s in human i<scp>PS</scp> and mouse <scp>ES</scp> cells",
        "id": "https://openalex.org/W1743052068",
        "cited_by_count": 67
      },
      "description": "These studies are unified by a central concern with the specificity and safety of CRISPR nucleases: how often they cut or nick unintended DNA or RNA targets, and what sequence features permit such off-target activity. Methodologically, they use systematic specificity profiling—mismatch and bulge-containing target libraries, predicted off-target site testing in cells, cleavage/nicking assays, and biochemical measurements such as PAM binding—to compare natural enzymes and engineered variants across Cas9, Cas12a, and Cas13 systems. Together, they show that CRISPR effectors vary widely in off-target behavior, that even “high-fidelity” or seemingly specific nucleases can retain unexpected collateral or mismatch-tolerant activity, and that guide/target context strongly shapes editing precision."
    },
