        "id": "https://openalex.org/W4323666400",
        "cited_by_count": 544
      },
      "description": "These papers are unified by concern over the safety of CRISPR/Cas genome editing, especially the detection, quantification, and mitigation of unintended genome alterations before therapeutic use. They synthesize preclinical and clinical evidence, comparing genome-wide assays, sequencing-based detection methods, and design/engineering strategies to reduce off-target cutting, while also emphasizing that “off-target” risk includes not only small sequence changes but also larger structural and vector-related integration events. Together, they frame off-target analysis as a central translational bottleneck for moving CRISPR from promising tool to reliable medicine."
